On 2007-10-11, Philip Potter <pgp@see.sig.invalid> wrote:
> Derek Ray wrote:
>> On 2007-10-11, Philip Potter <pgp@see.sig.invalid> wrote:
>>> However just as you don't think "realism" is the right term, I don't 
>>> think "internal consistency" is either. Firstly, it gives no clues about 
>>> what to do before a ruleset exists: either before the game exists, or 
>>> before a significant new feature is added. 
>> 
>> Then one can use external consistency; things which are added should be
>> relatively similar to other things that currently exist in the game.
>> 
>> Beyond that, one can draw from the same source material that other bits
>> in the game drew from.
>
> And one of those sources is reality.

That should be the last "familiar" source chosen.  Introducing reality
always leads to other problems, since reality is _the_ most complicated
source material available.

>>> Secondly, if all eggs were 
>>> readable but not eatable, that'd be consistent - but it wouldn't be 
>>> intuitive.
>> 
>> Unless you've led up to it properly in the game by having someone direct
>> the player to 'read the egg', or other gameplay mechanics that should be
>> used when introducing non-intuitive features.
>> 
>> Once you get past _that_, there's not actually a problem.
>
> But you don't have to provide any such lead for players to eat eggs - 
> they do so naturally, because it's realistic to eat eggs.

But you don't HAVE to make eggs edible, is my point; and you can make it
work within the game.  Reality is not the be-all, end-all of sources for
material, and starting from reality is generally only a good idea when
there _isn't_ any other source material available.  In this case, there
isn't any fantasy material that says "chicken eggs are inedible" (except,
perhaps, for Zork, but _then_ the intuitive feature would be to make
sure the player is aware that this egg is not made of the same material
as 'real' eggs), so you can then go back and say "what's the most common
and/or well-known reference point for eggs?"  Turns out that's reality,
so go ahead and use eggs from reality.

